  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Doesn't seem to have been suggested before but someone may have suggested alternate methods to achieve the same end goal.\nSince the Udon player audio override options allow for more fine-grained control over when, how, and for whom the audio is overridden, the additional ability to override the perceived origin of player audio has a few use cases:\nIn a team-based game where players need to communicate but may end up being spread far apart, teammates who are out of earshot could have their audio coming from near/inside of the player's head.\nPlayers communicating through remote \"video chat\" stations in different parts of a map. Players near one station would have their audio coming from the screen for players near the other station.\nIn some sort of vehicular competition game, spectators would hear the participant's voices broadcasted via loudspeaker from their respective vehicles, but the participants would be able to trash-talk each other via \"radios\" without giving away their position.\nGranted, the \"utility\" for most if not all of these use cases could probably be granted via the existing audio override options, but would lack the immersion of directional audio (unless I'm failing to properly comprehend their power)",
